+++ b/AGENT.md
@@ -13,3 +13,33 @@ line per lesson, so the next pass starts where you left off.
 
 ## Learned
 
+- `tokenize`: `text.toLowerCase().match(/[a-z0-9]+(?:'[a-z0-9]+)*/g) ?? []`
+  naturally satisfies "apostrophe inside a word stays" — the apostrophe only
+  joins two matches when it has word characters on both sides, so leading,
+  trailing or standalone apostrophes/quotes are dropped without special-casing.
+- `frequencies`: build directly on `tokenize` (call it, don't re-implement word
+  splitting) and count with a plain `Map`, using `counts.get(word) ?? 0` — `Map`
+  preserves insertion order natively, so first-seen order falls out for free
+  and there's no need to special-case an empty/no-word input beyond an empty
+  `Map`. Non-string input throws because `tokenize` already throws.
+- `tokenize`: the reviewer's acceptance cases treat a typographic apostrophe
+  (U+2019, `’`) the same as a straight `'` inside a word, and expect the
+  output word to use the straight apostrophe (`it’s` and `it's` both tokenize
+  to `"it's"`). The word regex must accept `['’]` between letters/digits,
+  and the matched substrings must then be normalized by replacing `’`
+  with `'` — `match()` returns the original characters, so without the
+  `.replace` step a typographic apostrophe survives into the token.
+- `topWords`: build on `frequencies` (spread its entries into an array) and
+  sort with a comparator that puts count descending first, then word
+  ascending as the tiebreak (`b[1] - a[1] || (a[0] < b[0] ? -1 : a[0] > b[0]
+  ? 1 : 0)`) — `Array.prototype.sort` is stable in Node but the explicit
+  alphabetical tiebreak is still required since insertion (first-seen) order
+  is not alphabetical order. `slice(0, n)` naturally returns fewer than `n`
+  pairs when there are fewer distinct words. Non-string input throws because
+  `frequencies` (via `tokenize`) already throws.
+- `summary`: a thin composition of the three earlier functions — `words` is
+  `tokenize(text).length`, `unique` is `frequencies(text).size`, `top` is
+  `topWords(text, 3)`. No new edge-case logic is needed: empty text falls out
+  as `{ words: 0, unique: 0, top: [] }` and non-string input throws because
+  `tokenize` (called first) already throws.
+
